course: dictionary
lesson: 11160
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Wind is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants. «The strong wind blew the kite high over the field.» Here wind is moving air, because the kite is pushed up by it.
explain: Wind can also mean breath. «I had to stop running to get my wind back.» Nobody is talking about air moving across the land here. When wind means turn or twist, it rhymes with "find". Say the sentence with your own meaning in it to check: "I had to stop running to get my breath back" still makes sense, so breath is right.
explain: Wind is also a verb, meaning to turn round and round or to bend and turn. «Wind the string round the stick.» «The path winds through the woods.» Swap in "turn" to check: "The path turns through the woods" still makes sense, so this wind means bend and turn.
fact: Wind means moving air. The Sun heats the Earth unevenly, and that makes the air move.
fact: Scientists measure wind with a wind vane, which shows the direction, and an anemometer, which measures the speed.
fact: Wind turbines use the wind to make electricity. This is clean, renewable energy.
fact: Wind, sunshine and flowing water keep coming back, so they are called renewable.
fact: The push of the wind grows very fast as it speeds up: wind twice as fast pushes about four times as hard. Strong winds can blow down trees, so stay inside in a storm.
exercise: «The strong wind blew the kite high over the field.» What does wind mean here? | A bit of breath | To turn a key | *Moving air across the land | To bend and turn
exercise: «I had to stop running to get my wind back.» What does wind mean here? | *Breath | Moving air | To twist something round and round | Gas in the stomach
exercise: «The path winds through the woods.» What does winds mean here? | Moving air | To turn a clock | *To bend and turn | To blow hard
test: Which meaning of wind is needed in «Wind the clock every morning»? | Gas in the stomach | Breath | Moving air | *To turn a key or handle so a clock works
test: Why do people build wind turbines? | To wind up a clock | To breathe more easily | *To make electricity from the wind | To measure rain
exam: What does wind mean in «After the race, Sam sat down to get his wind back»? | To turn round and round | *Breath | Moving air | Electricity
